- * The clock outputs are gate clocks.  The gate geometry (register offset and
- * bit) is driver data; the DT ABI is only the index, see
- * include/dt-bindings/clock/hisilicon,luofu-crg.h.  Every gate is currently
- * parented on the APB clock: the real parent clocks (the GEMAC/LSW PLL
- * outputs) are part of the clock-tree work that is not done yet, and rates
- * must not be "fixed" by editing the gates before that model exists.
+ * The clock outputs are modelled as one provider with four groups of clocks:
+ *
+ *  - 16 gate clocks (0x14/0x20), the consumer ABI plane;
+ *  - the two PLLs (CPU at 0x198, LSW at 0x1e0), READ-ONLY;
+ *  - the two muxes (0x138), READ-ONLY.
+ *
+ * The gate geometry (register offset and bit) is driver data; the DT ABI is
+ * only the index, see include/dt-bindings/clock/hisilicon,luofu-crg.h.  Each
+ * gate is parented on the real input clock of its leaf block, decoded from
+ * the vendor devicetree (MISC-SOC-BLOCKS.md section 5.2): the APB gates on
+ * apb_clk, the PCIe gates on apb_clk, the five GEMAC gates and the MDIO gate
+ * on gemacN_clk, the LSW data-path/PFE gates on lsw_dp_clk/lsw_pfe_clk and
+ * the PIE gate on ahb_clk.  The provider's input clocks are named by the
+ * devicetree node's clock-names and resolved through of_clk_get_parent_name(),
+ * so the clock tree is described in the DT instead of being guessed from a
+ * global name.
+ *
+ * The PLLs and muxes are registered but deliberately read-only:
+ *
+ *  - PLL rates are derived from the control words on demand (recalc_rate and
+ *    is_enabled only); there is no .set_rate.  The vendor write protocol
+ *    (busy bit, re-lock poll) is decoded but the encoding of the feedback
+ *    words has not been verified by a register readback on this board, and a
+ *    wrong store can lock up the CPU.  Confirm the rate equation on hardware
+ *    before any write support is added; see the comments at the PLL ops.
+ *  - The CPU mux (0x138 bit 3) selects the CPU clock source between the two
+ *    PLLs; it must never be writable.  The efuse mux (0x138 bit 1) is written
+ *    by the vendor efuse driver only while programming efuses; no efuse write
+ *    path is ported (the cells are one-way fences and key material), so this
+ *    driver does not accept set_parent for either mux.
  *
  * The reset provider uses a two-cell specifier: the CRG page register offset
  * of the reset word and the bit number.  The offsets are restricted to the
  * three reset words above so that a devicetree reset specifier can never
- * write the watchdog, soft-reset, mux or PLL words.  The bit polarity
- * (assert sets the bit, deassert clears it) has not been exercised on
- * hardware: no in-tree consumer uses the provider yet, and the vendor's
- * reset-controller description has the opposite polarity.  Confirm the
- * polarity on hardware before adding reset consumers.
+ * write the watchdog, soft-reset, mux or PLL words.  The reset class is
+ * active-low: a set bit releases the line (patch 014).
  *
  * Deliberately not implemented here:
  *   - reboot/reset of the SoC (the vendor path writes sysctrl/bootreg
  *     registers that are not understood; a wrong sequence would wedge the
- *     board, so arch/arm/mach-hisilicon/luofu.c also leaves .restart unset);
- *   - the soft-reset magic words, the mux at 0x138 and the two PLLs
- *     (0x198/0x1e0): they are outside the gate/reset model and some of them
- *     are dangerous to write.
+ *     board, so arch/arm/mach-hisilicon/luofu.c also leaves .restart unset
+ *     and the hisilicon,luofu-reboot driver keeps the provisional path);
+ *   - the soft-reset magic words and any write to the reboot/resume words;
+ *   - any write to the PLL control words or the mux word.
  */
 
